  title = 'AI Is Leaving the Chat Box: Robots, Voice Agents, Health',
  excerpt = 'FLUX 3 targets robot learning as ChatGPT and Claude expand voice agents, while Health in ChatGPT reaches eligible U.S. users.',
  content = '<p><strong>AI is moving beyond the chat box: into robots, hands-free work, and personal health context.</strong></p><p>Today''s updates point to the same shift from different directions. Black Forest Labs is pushing visual models toward real-world robotics, OpenAI and Anthropic are making agents easier to direct by voice, and ChatGPT Health is expanding access to connected health information.</p><h2>1. FLUX 3 brings visual AI closer to robots</h2><p>Black Forest Labs has introduced FLUX 3, a multimodal model family designed to work across image, video, and audio. The company says its research work includes an action model with mimic robotics, aimed at robot-learning and physical-world tasks; FLUX 3 Video is launching in early access. This is not a claim that a chatbot can run a factory tomorrow. It is a meaningful step toward models that can perceive and act on richer visual information.</p><p><a href="https://bfl.ai/blog/flux-3" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Black Forest Labs'' FLUX 3 announcement</a>.</p><h2>2. Voice is becoming an agent control layer</h2><p>OpenAI has added Voice to Work and Codex in the ChatGPT desktop app. On eligible macOS and Windows accounts, people can speak to start tasks, ask for progress, interrupt work, and coordinate tasks using the tools and permissions available to the selected experience.</p><p>Anthropic is making the same bet in Claude: its voice experience now reaches its more capable Opus and Sonnet options, while connected-app capabilities bring email, calendars, and other work tools into the agent workflow. The important change is not voice as a novelty; it is voice as a way to steer longer-running work without returning to a keyboard for every instruction.</p><p><a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001275-chatgpt-work-and-codex" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read OpenAI''s Work and Codex guide</a> · <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12138966-release-notes" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Anthropic''s release notes</a>.</p><h2>3. Health in ChatGPT expands in the U.S.</h2><p>OpenAI is rolling out Health in ChatGPT to eligible logged-in U.S. users aged 18 and over on Free, Go, Plus, and Pro plans. With permission, users can connect supported medical records and Apple Health data, then ask context-aware questions about results, appointments, activity, and wellness trends. It is available on web and iOS, rolls out gradually, and is designed to support—not replace—professional medical care.</p><p><a href="https://openai.com/index/health-in-chatgpt/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read OpenAI''s Health in ChatGPT announcement</a>.</p><h2>Why this matters</h2><p>These are different products, but the direction is shared: AI is being positioned less as a page where you type a question and more as a system that sees, listens, and works across real tools and data. That makes permissions, privacy, and human review more important—not less.</p><h2>Editorial note</h2><p>This independent report was prompted by the July 24 Superhuman newsletter and verified against the linked company sources. The cover image is an original Free Prompt Base illustration, not a product screenshot.</p>',
  source_url = 'https://bfl.ai/blog/flux-3',
  cover_image = 'https://freepromptbase.com/cdn/cms/news/ai-next-interface-july-2026.webp',
  read_time = '3 min read',
